# Contributing Workflow

Canonical owner for day-to-day git collaboration. Changelog format: `changelog.mdc`. Tagging / GitHub releases: `github-release.mdc`.

## Commits

- Use **conventional commits**: `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `perf`, `ci`.
- Subject: imperative, concise; body explains why when non-obvious.
- Reference issues when applicable (GitHub `#123` / GitLab `#123`).
- Keep commits focused — do not mix unrelated refactors with feature work.

## Branches

- One purpose per branch (single feature, fix, or chore).
- Prefer short names: `feat/<topic>`, `fix/<topic>`, `chore/<topic>`.
- Rebase or merge from the default branch before opening/updating a PR/MR when history is noisy.

## Pull / Merge Requests

- Small, reviewable diffs with a clear purpose: one PR moves one small chunk of change, never a whole plan.
- Description: what changed, why, how to test; link issues.
- Update the description when behavior changes during review.
- Do not include secrets, large generated artifacts, or unrelated lockfile churn unless the change requires it.

## Findings workflow (bugs / issues / improvements)

1. Create a GitHub issue per finding **before** fixing (`gh issue create`).
2. Fix on a focused branch.
3. Open a PR that closes the issue (`Closes #N`).
4. Merge the PR once checks pass (`gh pr merge`).

Large findings: split the work into small PRs.

- Several PRs may reference one issue. Use `Part of #N` in each PR and `Closes #N` only in the last one; list the chunks as a checklist in the issue.
- Or split the finding into several issues, one per chunk, each closed by its own PR.
- After each PR merges, update its issue: tick the checklist item, note the result (for example measured timings) and list the next chunk.

Exception: changelog-only PRs need no issue.

## Required checks before ask-to-merge

Prefer locally:

```bash
make format
make lint
make behave   # or targeted feature files
make security # when touching adapters, auth, crypto, or network code
```

Or `make check` / `make ci` for the full gate.

## Changelog & release handoff

- **Do not** update changelog every session, commit, or PR by default.
- Skip changelog for Cursor rules, `AGENTS.md`, internal chores, tests-only, and non-consumer docs unless the user says otherwise.
- Write or edit changelog **only** when the user explicitly asks for a changelog, version bump, or release.
- When asked: follow `changelog.mdc`. Do **not** create tags / GitHub releases until the draft is accepted — then `github-release.mdc`.
